    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** leads the charge with an elite 80.43 score, serving as our primary engine despite facing a slight efficiency drag from the opposing catcher\u2019s quick pop time. **Mickey Moniak** is our secondary force at 75.75, benefiting immensely from a massive 14% platoon boost against Alcantara. Meanwhile, **Seiya Suzuki** presents high-upside value at 69.53, where favorable wind conditions provide the necessary lift to push him into our preferred start tier. These three anchors effectively weaponize our lineup against middle-of-the-road pitching, capitalizing on park factors that favor the hitters in their respective matchups.\n\nTactically, we are leaning into platoon advantages to maximize our output. **Max Muncy** (68.41) and **Henry Bolte** (65.40) represent our most efficient leverage plays, both benefiting from the 17% SP skill adjustment provided by Lauer. While **Zach McKinstry** (48.41) carries a lower efficiency score, his inclusion is a calculated gamble on his SB potential, specifically targeting Weathers\u2019 slow delivery to manufacture stolen bases. Conversely, **Cole Young** was the most difficult exclusion; he flirted with the starting threshold at 46.51 but ultimately lacked the high-leverage platoon or lineup position (7th) to displace our higher-ceiling options.\n\nRisk remains localized primarily around our catchers and lower-order bats. We are monitoring **Gabriel Moreno** (63.83) as he navigates a matchup against Mahle, where any late-inning defensive substitutions could cap his plate appearances. We are also wary of **James Wood** (62.24); while his underlying xwOBA is elite, the presence of a high-hold opposing pitcher creates an efficiency floor that warrants close observation throughout the day. With **Dalton Rushing** benched due to a sub-40 efficiency score, our focus remains on protecting our daily cap while squeezing every possible point from these high-probability platoon spots.",
